<p>In 2008, the occupants of a Zagreb apartment building proposed converting it into a condominium. The only resident who failed to agree was Hedviga Golik, a former nurse who had once occupied a one-room apartment in the building&#8217;s attic. Withdrawn, temperamental, and alienated from her family, Golik had not been seen in decades and was thought to have left the area. A dispute among the other tenants had prevented any of them from laying claim to the apartment.</p>
<p>When three representatives broke open the door that May, Golik&#8217;s body was lying on the bed, wrapped in blankets, a teacup by her side. She had died in 1966 and lain there for 42 years. The building superintendent, a former boyfriend, had been providing the unit rent-free, and the building&#8217;s architect had routinely paid the electricity bill.</p>
<p>She was buried the next day. A police spokesperson said, &#8220;So far, we have no idea how it is possible that someone officially reported missing so long ago was not found before in the same apartment she used to live in.&#8221;</p>
